<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Generalife</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Generalife is the summer palace and garden estate of the Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, on the hill beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. Built in the early fourteenth century, probably under Muhammad III, and redecorated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, it is known for the Court of the Water Channel, a long pool lined with fountains and flowerbeds. With the Alhambra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albaicin">Albaicín</a> it is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/generalife.jpg" alt="Generalife" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patio_de_la_Acequia_(Generalife)_-_DSC07863_(slightly_cropped_and_sharpened).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Generalife is most often derived from the Arabic jannat al-‘arīf, variously rendered Garden of the Architect, Garden of the Artist, Garden of the Gnostic or Garden of the Flautist. Robert Irwin finds this unlikely and considers the true origin unclear. An inscription by Ibn al-Jayyab names the palace Dar al-Mamlakat as-Sa'ida, the House of the Felicitous Kingdom.</p><p>In the Nasrid period it was an almunia, a country villa used both as a private retreat and as a farming estate, a kind of royal estate known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> since the Umayyad period. Muhammad III contributed to its early decoration and added the mirador chamber in the northern pavilion. According to an inscription it was remodelled in 1319 under Isma'il I, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and court poet Ibn al-Jayyab composed the inscriptions around the entrance to the Salón Regio. Yusuf III remodelled the southern sections of the palace in the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The gardens occupy three large terraces on the hillside, and the palace stands on a fourth terrace above them. Its core is the Patio de la Acequia, a courtyard measuring 12.8 by 48.7 metres, divided into four elongated flowerbeds with a water channel along its long axis. This four-part layout has its roots in the Persian chahar bagh garden. A small mirador, or lookout chamber, projects from the western wall, and is probably the earliest known mirador in Nasrid architecture. Pavilions stand at the north and south ends, and the north pavilion holds the Salón Regio.</p><p>Water came from the Acequia Real, which draws on the Darro River about 6.1 kilometres east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. Above the Patio de la Sultana rises the Water Stairway, a four-flight staircase whose balustrades are carved with channels. The Nasrid rulers could reach the palace from the Alhambra by a private covered passage that crossed the ravine between them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> the Catholic Monarchs added an upper story to the northern pavilion in 1494, and the Patio de la Sultana was redesigned in the 16th century. The Venetian traveller Andrea Navagero described the Generalife in 1524–26, before most later Spanish changes, and modern scholars use his account to reconstruct its original appearance. Leopoldo Torres Balbás and Francisco Prieto Moreno rearranged much of the gardens between 1931 and 1951. A fire in 1958 damaged the northern parts and allowed excavations that recorded the Nasrid paths and soil. Since 1984 the Generalife has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Generalife” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Poems by Ibn Zamrak and other court poets are inscribed on its walls, making the building a book of Arabic verse; together with the <b>Generalife</b> gardens and the Albaicín quarter it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
